Date:     Fri, 5 Feb 93 9:27:53 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: Wal-Mart, Malls, etc.

Ah, the inevitable newbies, and the inevitable game I play with Perry of
"Good Cop, Bad Cop":

Andy Wilson's essential claim appears to be that rural America has been
"dehumanized" by such as Wal-Mart.  He blames these and malls for the
demise of the downtown area of his hometown and others like it.

Dave Worley comes a bit closer with the claim that Walton exploited
rural areas.

But, actually, you're both wrong :-)!  Malls, Wal-Mart, and the decline of
downtowns are related, but not so simply.

First, Wal-Mart.  What did Sam Walton's company really compete with, and how
did he win out over these other firms?  Wal-Mart's main competitor was K-Mart,
with Sears also in the running.  Unless Andy Wilson had these in mind as
the bastions of downtown rural culture, which I strongly doubt, then Sam
isn't much to blame for its demise.  He won the competition through high
integration, both vertical and horizontal, computerization.  As soon as
items were sold, new ones were automatically ordered to replace them on
the shelves from distributors and/or manufacturers.  This is also practiced
in at least one record store, according to what the owner told me.

I base the above on testimony of a former K-mart manager and an acquaintance
whose father worked as a financial consultant to Sears.  Sears had the
wherewithal to do what Wal-Mart did, by the way, but didn't have the
management.

Dale or Andy also said "I don't think that the mere fact that someone is wealthy
should command any more or less respect for them than anyone else... that
does not make them out to be heroes..."

Have you any respect for someone because of the mere fact that they win
an Olympic competition?  Most people do, correctly.  If someone plays
fair and wins, they've earned our respect.  The team that won the Superbowl
certainly gets some of their due for their accomplishment.

So, why this disrespect for those who win economic competition by playing
fair while bettering the condition of their customers in the process?  The
sports competitions are mere zero-sum games; only one party wins.  In
business, both parties win.  Surely this should accord the greater
respect to the winners of business competition!

Predistinarianism hasn't anything to do with this other than as an accident
of history and religion.

Wal-Mart had little to do with the demise of rural downtowns.  Malls,
however, may well have, for good reason.

Dale doesn't like Malls, but I do.  They impress me as underrated examples
of de facto privatization of much of what is taken for granted as having
necessarily to be provided by the State: roads, security, walkways, parking,
etc.  The stores in them tend to be commercial chains, but this is also
an accidental feature, not a necessary one - but not necessarily a bad
one, either.  Industrial and residential parks are similar.

Malls have some inherent advantages over traditional downtowns: they
aren't saddled with public roads, security, sidewalks, parking, etc. :-).
At least around here all the politicians have contracted some disease
that makes them put these public goods that statists always trot out to
support their claim that we must have tyranny into "deferred maintenance,"
which means neglect after being translated into English from bureaucratese.

The public roads are filled with potholes, congested, the traffic signals
and rules Byzantine; the police have a much easier time of it committing
crime against violators of the superstitious dietary laws they like to
call the War On Drugs than actually interfere with the chosen professions
of violent criminals; the sidewalks are filthy, in disrepair, and strewn
about with bums (truly homeless people can get into shelters or break
into buildings in our Squatters' Paradises) whose alcholic breath and
urine make merely walking by odious; and parking is impossible during
daytime in commercial areas and residential ones by night.

These problems are worst in urban areas, not rural ones, but they exist
to some degree wherever the Public Sector does.  If Malls are driving
the traditional downtown commercial districts of rural America out of
business, then these may well be the reasons why.  Strangely enough, though,
all the rural areas I know of with malls have remaining downtown sections,
too.  The malls result from growth into niches not already occupied
by firms, not from putting thriving stores out of business.

Aside from these major points, some minor ones remain to be addressed.
Almost no discussion of the economy seems complete until someone brings
up the subjects of S&Ls and Michael Milken.  Milken has been done to
death, but Dale's wrong about him inventing junk bonds.  He didn't.  He
innovated their use in a way that also refutes Dale about why corporate
management is so static.

In the 1960s, anti-takeover legislation was passed in the USA (I'd be
interested to know what the situation is in other countries) to the effect
that once you acquired 5% of a firm's stock, you had to publicly declare
takeover intent.  Your opponents then had some weeks to prepare their
defense, marshal resources, etc.  This led to enormous marshalling of
resources by corporate raiders, who had to prepare to move as quickly as
possible after their waiting period was up.

If you know that, say, Ross Perot was preparing a takeover attempt, you
could buy some of the stock on the cheap, sell it during the fight, and
make a killing.  This is what's called "insider trading."  It's wholly a
creature of the State, not a market failure.

The effect of this legislation was to decrease takeovers and increase
managerial stability, to its great pleasure.  That sort of anti-competitive
rule is what they rent political power for, after all, and why they
finance political campaigns.

Milken figured out how to use bonds that these managers called "junk" to
quickly finance takeover attempts.  In so doing, he was the greatest
threat to management than any union was during the 1980s.  He also created
millions of jobs in the process.

Dale's analysis of the S&L crisis is about right, and his analysis of
corporate management and the persecution of political prisoner Milken.
I just wanted to clarify some of the causes of the conditions.

