Subject: NANOTIME VALUES

I have been enjoying the thread started by Ron Hale-Evans considering 
possible stores of value in a near or post singularity world. I believe that 
serious consideration of this issue will lead to profitable investments in 
the near future. 

Consider the following thought experiment (or not). The year is 1997 and 
Milton Friedman is seriously ill. He has considered, and rejected cryonics 
for what seem to extropian-types to be unclear reasons. After it is clear he 
will not make arrangements, a wealthy extropian (perhaps a wearable computer 
magnate) offers to pay for the procedure. Still no go - he does not want to 
live any more (probably an irrationality caused by his ill health - which 
means its probably always a better idea to approach likely candidates while 
they're still healthy). So now, the mystery extropian ups the ante. He says 
something to the effect that, "Okay, not only will I pay for it, I'll donate 
1 million frns to the Friedman Foundation, and since I respect your right to 
not be revived, we won't revive you....though we will try to recover as many 
memories as possible, to construct a "memory movie" of your life". Milton is 
still hesitant, but after discussing who would edit such a movie, what kinds 
of memories and specific ones would be off limits, and who would get to view 
it, he relents. And the mystery extropian didn't even have to threaten to 
give the million frns to Milton's least favorite cause, if he did not enter 
into this contract. 

Question: Would the owner of a well preserved celebrity brain have an asset 
that would appreciate even before the technology to extract memories exists, 
in anticipation of its arrival?


Doug


