Date: Fri, 5 Feb 1993 08:58:44 -0500 (EST)
From: Harry Shapiro <habs@Panix.Com>
Subject: Meta: Improving the List, please read

This is to let the general list know that I am working on some
ideas to improve the list. Working on this with me is Ray (rjc),
Derek, Sasha, and others.

WARNING: This is  n o t  a "call" for discussion or debate. Feedback
	 sent to exi@panix.com is welcome.

This is a "work in process." I am not opening up a debate about
implementation concepts, feature requests, reasons it will or will not
work, etc.. Private comments are welcome including praise.
Comments should be sent to exi@panix.com.

FIRST, I am not saying that the list is "broken" but I feel that for
us to continue to grow the list membership, some changes will have to
be made. 60 to 80% of those leaving the list state that the volume of
the traffic is "too much" and cite that as their reason for leaving. I
also feel the general issue of information over-load will effect more
and more people, and this effort is a real example of how we
(Extropians) and actually do something. (Helping people get to our
ideas without blowing them away with the volume.)

SECOND, the main list (real-time, reflected) and the 50k real-time
digest will not change and will continue to be available in their
current format. (Except for message numbers, see item 1.)

THIRD, I have been warned about adding to much complexity to the code
and everything will be well-tested, AND any new code will exist
separately from the Main List and the 50k real-time Digest.

PLANS, We are working on adding the following:

1) Adding a message number header to each out-going message. This will
appear on all messages across all delivery methods. It will be used for
several purposes including helping GNU staff (if willing) to track
down messages stuck (and thus repeating) in the sendmail "buffer."

2) Allowing "threads" to be subscribed and unsubscribe by Prefix.
That is one reason why it is useful and important to use them.

3) David Friedman, Sasha, Robin, Carl (and others) have talked about a
way to have people "tell/talk" about what messages they like, and then
have some algorithm determine other messages (or books or TV shows,
etc) that they might like. One method of doing this is to use input
from each person to find a Vector of their preferences. Using that
Vector you could find Vectors of other people who were close. One
could assume that if your Vector is close to someone elses that you
would like messages they like. The more feedback you provide the
better the system should work. We are working on creating such a
system.  (see 3b).

3a) The value to each list member is that the more messages they rate
the better the system would work for them. It would also generally
help other list members (see item 4), but the purpose for rating is
firmly rooted in self interest.

3b) By allowing list members to rate any desired message on a scale of
-10 to +10, we hope to have a database we can use to implement one or
more algorithms as described in item 3 (above).

4) Allow list messages to be subscribed or unsubscribed by poster.
For example get messages by "foo" but NOT "bar".

4a) Allow list messages to be subscribed by combinations of posters,
prefixes, and raw rating data. For example messages with the prefix
"Nano:," sent by "foo" and rated by "bar" greater than 4.

5) We will create a simple input lang. for sending instructions for
doing this.

6) The output from these various subscriptions will be batched
to avoid hogging FSF resources.

7) A major design criteria is that we shouldn't get kicked off "GNU."

8) A short archive will be kept (several days) so missed messages
can be rerequested, best of digests can be created, etc.

9) A telnet socket (probably PM use only) will be available so that
individuals can create local front ends to our system. This will be the
last part complete and maybe dropped if it consumes to much resources.

/Harry

