Date: Fri, 5 Feb 93 08:37:59 CST
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: ECON/NANO: A short question

In response to the quotation "I think that trade will break down
a lot when we can personally manufacture all we need" I will
respond with a couple of comments.  The historical trend has been
towards more and more movement of materials and goods around the
world.  Nanotech will make building transportation systems 
as much cheaper as anything else.  Also, the trade will shift
from goods as we delive them now, to the next higher production
level.  In the case of woodworking (a hobby), you shift from
buying furniture for your house to buying tools, hardware, and
specialty lumber.  In a nanotech era, you buy assembler seed
kits, special input materials, and deisgn databases.  Or do you
think just any backyard has enough copper to wire a house?  There
are still geological variations in materials that have to be
contended with.

Dani Eder

