>Could we perhaps agree that a cash bet would be zero sum?

Imagine you and I pick our favorite football teams, and agree that the
loser will pay the winner $100 next year depending on who got closer
to the superbowl.  To apply your definition, we now have to decide
what are "the goods and cash involved in the transactions" at this
moment in time, in order to compare their value now and in a year.  If
you presume that to honor this bet we must each place a crisp $100
bill under our mattresses, then yes, by construction the sum of the
market value (denominated in dollars) of the assets is constant.  

But this isn't what we would do at all.  We would each instead set
aside an amount now which we expect to be worth $100 next year, after
accruing value in our usual investment portfolio.  If we really
didn't trust each other, we might each place a certain amount in a
bank account, which the bank guarentees will be worth $100 next year.
If this bank account is "the goods and cash [now] involved in the
transactions", then this bet is positive sum, according to your
definition.

Folks are just so used to living in state-run monetary systems, where
the money loses value over time, that they think there is something
fundamental about using U.S. dollars as a unit of account for the
purpose of economic analysis.  But decent private money would pay
interest, relative to state money.

>Then, I will ask how the futures transaction example can become non-zero-
>sum.  Recall that my analysis suggested that the payoff to me was
>$450-x, and the payoff to you was x-$450, if the market price of gold is
>$x.  Was that analysis wrong?  Isn't this clearly a zero-sum transaction?

Again, to apply your definition we have to ask what are the current
assets now "involved in the transaction".  Depending on our investment
behavior, this could be all sorts of things, but lets focus on what
assets would be required to guarentee that we could hold up our end of
the deal: "One year from now, Hal will deliver one ounce of gold to
Robin in exchange for $450".  For me it is the present value of $450
in one year, and for you it is the present price of gold.  Since gold
goes up in price on average, and since I can earn interest on money,
the present value of the assets averages less than their value in one
year, values here calculated in dollars.  And this is why real futures
contracts do give competitive rates of return.  

Robin Hanson

