>On the otherhand, I do not understand this libertarian infatuation against 
>the use of force.  Any libertarian who really doesn't use the threat of
>violence (by him/herself or the state) is fooling him/herself.  I don't
>care how well you can produce or how innovative you are, if you can't 
>force me to pay for your product, I'll take it for free.

I think perhaps you misunderstand the libertarian's relation to force.  The
standard lib formula is not that force should not be used, but that force
should not be INITIATED.  In other words, the only legitimate uses of force
are in self-defense and in response to another's INITIATION of force.  So
"If you touch my car, I'll blow your head off" (exaggerated) is OK, but "If you don't
give me your money, ...." is right out.  

I think at the heart of any serious libertarian's self-governance is an
implicit threat of violence.  Violence may be the last refuge of the
incompetent, but primarily because it is often the last refuge, period.

Next to lastly, let me note that I think the final statement in the above
paragraph is misstated.  As a libertarian, I can't force you to pay for my
product, if you don't take it.  But I can use any means at my disposal to
prevent you from taking it without paying.

Lastly, as a utilitarian libertarian, I have to concur with David Friedman
that there are instances where, practically, a small instance of initiation
of force is necessary to prevent a much larger and more grievous instance.
I refer you to "The Machinery of Freedom" for more details including
examples.  Note that some might argue that this opens a can of worms, but I
would argue that this particular can was never sealed in the first place.

